{
"A minor official and teacher who wanted to restore the virtue behind Zhou ritual. His sayings, collected by students as the Analects, shaped East Asian ethics and education for two thousand years.": "40adcc918c6a",
"A proud and obscure writer who taught that the world is an ever-living fire, always changing according to measure, and that most people fail to grasp the order, the logos, that runs through everything.": "094cc2aa4b6b",
"Confucius reciprocity, the Buddhas compassion for all beings, and later the Stoic idea of a world citizen all reach beyond ones own clan or city. Rules began to be justified by reasons that apply to everyone.": "c4c91629b277",
"Confucius reformed ritual instead of rejecting it.": "92c747440d9a",
"In 1949 the German philosopher Karl Jaspers pointed out something that every student of world history eventually notices. In a few centuries around 500 BCE, and in three regions that had little or no contact with one another, people began to think about the world and themselves in a new way. In China there were Confucius and Laozi and, a little later, Mozi, Mencius, and Zhuangzi. In India there were the sages of the Upanishads, the Buddha, and Mahāvīra, the founder of Jainism. In Greece there were the Milesians of the last lesson, then Heraclitus, Parmenides, and finally Socrates and Plato. Jaspers called this period the Axial Age, the time on which later history turned as on an axis.": "17146dae8dce",
"In China, the Zhou dynastys rituals had kept order for centuries, but by Confucius time the Zhou kings were powerless and the states fought one another. Confucius did not reject ritual. He asked what made it worth keeping, and found the answer in human virtue. When his student Zigong asked for one word to guide a whole life, Confucius replied, in James Legges translation, Is not RECIPROCITY such a word? What you do not want done to yourself, do not do to others. In Greece, the old poets gods were doubted by Xenophanes, and Heraclitus taught that this world, which is the same for all, no one of gods or men has made; but it was ever, is now, and ever shall be an ever-living Fire. Three different idioms, ritual reformed, ritual transcended, ritual replaced by nature, all expressed one change. People were now looking for an order they could understand.": "e7e5afa31efa",
"In India, the Vedic religion of fire sacrifices was already ancient. The Upanishads, composed by forest-dwelling teachers over several centuries, turned inward. The Katha Upanishad tells of a boy, Nachiketas, who is granted three wishes by Death himself and uses the third to ask, in Swami Paramanandas translation, There is this doubt regarding what becomes of a man after death. Some say he exists, others that he does not exist. Death begs him to choose something else, and the boy refuses. The Buddha and Mahāvīra went further and rejected the authority of the Vedas altogether. In his first sermon, in Bhikkhu Sujatos translation, the Buddha described a path between two extremes: Indulgence in sensual pleasures, which is low, and indulgence in self-mortification, which is painful.": "763f52df3fc8",
"In all three places, inherited ritual was no longer simply performed. It was reformed by Confucius, transcended by the Buddha and the Upanishads, and replaced by nature in Greece. Each time someone asked what it was for.": "2ccf276ea49c",
"Is not RECIPROCITY such a word? What you do not want done to yourself, do not do to others.": "9828a6c49402",
"Jaspers called it the Axial Age because later history turned on it.": "1f1a5dacd134",
"Legge translated shu as reciprocity.": "0127ea45be79",
"Siddhattha Gotama left a comfortable life, practiced severe austerities, rejected them, and taught a middle way. His four noble truths treat suffering as a problem with a cause and a cure.": "354b086e8fad",
"Some scholars doubt the whole idea. The age stretches over six hundred years, which is long for a coincidence. Important thinkers, such as Zarathustra in Iran and the Hebrew prophets, fit it only awkwardly, and earlier civilizations, Egypt and Mesopotamia, had wisdom literature of their own. The concept may say more about how modern Europeans wanted to see world history than about the ancient world. These are fair objections, and this course treats the Axial Age as a useful lens, not a law of history. Even so, the lens shows something true. The questions that still define philosophy, how to live, what is real, how we can know, what we owe one another, were first asked in recognizably modern form in these three places, at roughly the same time, by people who had never heard of one another.": "f2b66622551e",
"Teachers who were neither priests nor officials gathered students, traveled between states, and founded schools that outlived them. The Academy, the Confucian school, and the Buddhist sangha are all products of this age.": "976f9de478af",
"The Buddha practiced severe austerities before rejecting them.": "4f1470040bec",
"The Upanishads sought a self that transcends ritual.": "208c3c585679",
"The great debater of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upanishad, who taught that the true self cannot be described by any positive term and can only be indicated by saying not this, not this.": "209dbc4f62bb",
"There is this doubt regarding what becomes of a man after death. Some say he exists, others that he does not exist.": "a06c1669562a",
"This course treats the Axial Age as a lens, not a law.": "7c983b0b07d8",
"This world, which is the same for all, no one of gods or men has made; but it was ever, is now, and ever shall be an ever-living Fire, with measures kindling, and measures going out.": "cb57006d5ae5",
"Urbanization brought merchants who could pay teachers.": "a0953bcd43ee",
"What these people shared was not a set of doctrines. Their answers differed enormously. What they shared was a turn of attention. Older religion in all three places centered on ritual, the correct performance of sacrifices that kept the gods or ancestors favorable. The new teachers asked what lay behind the ritual and whether it mattered at all. They asked what a good person is, what the self is, why there is suffering, and what is real beneath appearances. They taught students, not only kings and priests, and their students argued with one another. In each place, an inherited way of life became something that could be examined.": "0710cce74864",
"Why then, and why there? Historians have offered several explanations. All three regions were becoming richer and more urban. The Lydians, Herodotus reports, were the first of men, so far as we know, who struck and used coin of gold or silver, and coinage appeared in India and China in the same centuries. Trade created merchants who traveled, compared customs, and had money for teachers. All three regions were also politically broken into rival states, the Greek cities, the Indian kingdoms of the Ganges plain, and the warring states of China. Rival courts competed for advisers, and a teacher who was unwelcome in one state could move to another. Wealth, writing, travel, and political competition together may have made room for people whose job was to think.": "9ee85bbf8a9f",
"Zarathustra fits the Axial Age only awkwardly.": "ed6924cdb2d6",
